import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a0 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CnTYizyl.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Configuration","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/deployment/configuration.md","filePath":"guide/deployment/configuration.md"}'),r={name:"guide/deployment/configuration.md"};function n(i,e,d,c,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="configuration" tabindex="-1">Configuration <a class="header-anchor" href="#configuration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Configuration&quot;">​</a></h1><p>All Hub services are configured via environment variables, managed by <code>envix</code> through each service&#39;s <code>ConfigModule</code>.</p><h2 id="common-variables" tabindex="-1">Common Variables <a class="header-anchor" href="#common-variables" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Common Variables&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These variables are shared across all services:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>PORT</code></td><td>No</td><td>HTTP server port (default varies by service)</td></tr><tr><td><code>AUTHUP_URL</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Authup identity provider URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>REDIS_URL</code></td><td>No</td><td>Redis connection URL (for pub/sub and caching)</td></tr><tr><td><code>AMQP_URL</code></td><td>No</td><td>RabbitMQ connection URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>COOKIE_PREFIX</code></td><td>No</td><td>Namespace prefixed onto the <code>access_token</code> cookie the authup middleware falls back to when a request carries no <code>Authorization</code> header (e.g. a bucket/file stream download). See <a href="#frontend-variables-client-ui">Frontend Variables</a> — must match client-ui&#39;s <code>COOKIE_PREFIX</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="message-delivery-rabbitmq" tabindex="-1">Message delivery (RabbitMQ) <a class="header-anchor" href="#message-delivery-rabbitmq" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Message delivery (RabbitMQ)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When <code>AMQP_URL</code> is set, commands to the worker, storage and telemetry services (build, distribute, bucket create, …) travel over durable RabbitMQ queues.</p><ul><li><strong>A failed or interrupted command is retried, up to 5 attempts.</strong> A handler error, or a redelivery after a consumer died mid-command (crash, lost connection, consumer timeout), re-publishes the command with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s). An exhausted command is dropped with an error log line naming its type and id. Before this, a redelivered command was dropped silently after its first attempt.</li><li><strong>The attempt counter is an AMQP header (<code>x-attempt</code>).</strong> Older versions kept it in the message body (<code>metadata.attempt</code>). While old and new replicas run side by side, a failing command may be retried more often than 5 times, because each version only reads its own counter, but it is never retried forever and never dropped early. No particular upgrade order is needed for this.</li><li><strong>The worker retries only failures that can go away on their own.</strong> A connection error, a 5xx/429 from the core or storage API, GitHub or the registry, or an unreachable or failing docker daemon is retried as above, and the analysis keeps its <code>started</code> status meanwhile. Anything else — a broken Dockerfile step, a 4xx, a missing registry — fails the build, distribution or check right away, as before. A retryable failure that persists through the 5th attempt is reported as failed, not dropped. Only a worker that dies on every attempt leaves a build or distribution <code>started</code>; server-core fails it once it is 15 minutes stale (see below). An error docker reports inside a build or push stream (e.g. a registry 503 during push) counts as permanent.</li><li><strong>Harbor registry commands go through the <code>registryCommands</code> queue</strong> (project link/relink/unlink, registry setup/cleanup/delete, webhook processing). They used to run inside server-core&#39;s request first and reach the queue only if that call threw. They are now only queued and run by the registry consumer, which retries a command that throws. The Harbor handlers still log a Harbor error and give up rather than throw, so a Harbor outage leaves the project unlinked, as before, until the next link (e.g. a node or registry project update). A node&#39;s robot account and registry credentials appear shortly after the node is created rather than with the response. Renaming a registry project&#39;s <code>externalName</code> is one relink command, so its unlink of the old Harbor project and its link of the new one cannot run out of order. Without a message bus server-core runs them in-process; a command sent before the registry consumer has started waits in the queue.</li><li><strong>Set RabbitMQ&#39;s <code>consumer_timeout</code> above your longest analysis or master-image build.</strong> A command is acknowledged only once its handler finishes. If a build outlasts the timeout (RabbitMQ default: 30 minutes), the broker closes the channel and redelivers the command. A replica still running it ignores the copy, but a redelivery that lands on another worker replica starts a second build.</li><li>Worker and storage outcome events (started, progress, finished, failed) go through one durable queue per kind (<code>analysisBuilderEvents</code>, <code>analysisDistributorEvents</code>, <code>masterImageBuilderEvents</code>, <code>masterImageSynchronizerEvents</code>, <code>storageBucketEvents</code>, <code>storageBucketFileEvents</code>). Each queue is a RabbitMQ <em>single active consumer</em> queue: one server-core replica applies its events in publish order, and another takes over if that replica goes away. Events published while server-core is down wait in the queue instead of being lost. A late <code>started</code>/<code>progress</code> event no longer moves a finished or failed process back to <code>started</code>.</li><li><strong>Upgrade the worker and storage services before server-core.</strong> An upgraded worker publishes its events to these queues, where they wait for the upgraded server-core. An upgraded server-core consumes only the queues, so it would miss events from a worker that still publishes the old way.</li><li><strong>Upgrading server-core across the <code>TaskManager</code> removal: stop the old replicas before new ones serve traffic.</strong> An old server-core links a storage bucket only to a create <em>it</em> issued, and it can keep the <code>storageBucketEvents</code> consumer slot until it disconnects. A bucket created by a new replica while an old one holds that slot stays unlinked, and the analysis has no bucket. If old and new replicas did overlap, run the storage check (<code>POST /analyses/:id/command</code> with <code>{ &quot;command&quot;: &quot;storageCheck&quot; }</code>) for every analysis created during the overlap. Old replies are fine in the other direction: a new server-core links every bucket created by an old one.</li><li><strong>Before upgrading again after a rollback, delete these six queues.</strong> They are durable, so a rollback leaves them on the broker, and the older version neither reads nor removes them. The next upgrade would apply whatever they still hold: a stale <code>started</code>/<code>progress</code> event is ignored, but a stale <code>finished</code>/<code>failed</code> event would overwrite the current status.</li><li><strong>Stuck builds and distributions are recovered by server-core.</strong> It re-checks every build/distribution that has been <code>starting</code>/<code>started</code> without an update for 15 minutes, every 5 minutes, and marks it <code>failed</code> if the image is not there. Running workers send a heartbeat every 5 minutes, so a long build is not affected (a step that sends no progress for 2 hours is treated as hung and stops the heartbeat). A hung step only makes the process stale: with a single worker replica the re-check queues behind the hung run, so it is recovered only by restarting the worker, running a second worker replica, or RabbitMQ&#39;s <code>consumer_timeout</code> ending the hung delivery. <strong>Upgrade the workers before server-core.</strong> An old worker sends no heartbeat and still decides staleness on its own: with a new server-core, the automatic re-check can reach an idle old worker replica, which then reports a healthy build or push with one step longer than 15 minutes as <code>failed</code>. A new worker with an old server-core is safe: it reports a stuck process as still running, so it stays stuck until server-core is upgraded too.</li><li><strong>Analysis buckets are deduplicated on upgrade.</strong> server-core now allows one analysis bucket per analysis and type, and server-storage one bucket per <code>(refType, refId, name)</code>. Their migrations clean up duplicates a repeated or concurrent bucket create may have left: server-core keeps the oldest link per analysis and type and deletes the newer ones (with their file rows), and server-storage clears <code>refType</code>/<code>refId</code> on every newer duplicate bucket. No storage bucket or file is deleted; a bucket whose link was dropped stays in server-storage as a ref-less orphan, which an operator can delete with <code>DELETE /buckets/:id</code>. A repeated bucket create command now answers with the existing bucket instead of creating another. <code>POST /buckets</code> no longer accepts a <code>refId</code> (400): only the service owning the referenced resource sets it, over the bus.</li><li>A lost broker connection is retried every 5 seconds (it was 60).</li><li><strong>Registry commands go through the queue.</strong> Linking, unlinking or relinking a project in Harbor, registry setup, cleanup and deletion, and Harbor webhook processing are published to the <code>registryCommands</code> queue and run on whichever server-core replica takes them, not inside the HTTP request that triggered them. Before, server-core ran the command itself first and fell back to the queue, so a failing command could run twice. Without <code>AMQP_URL</code> the command still runs inside the same server-core process. Renaming a registry project sends one relink command. Two commands for the same project sent by separate requests (an unlink, then a link) can run on two replicas at once, so wait for the first to finish before sending the second.</li><li>Failure events carry the error&#39;s <code>name</code>, <code>message</code> and <code>code</code> instead of <code>{}</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="authup-upgrade-to-beta-68" tabindex="-1">Authup upgrade to beta.68 <a class="header-anchor" href="#authup-upgrade-to-beta-68" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Authup upgrade to beta.68&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Upgrade the Authup server and all Hub services together. Hub now requires Authup <code>1.0.0-beta.68</code> and reads <code>GET /authorization</code> using the service&#39;s own <code>CLIENT_ID</code> / <code>CLIENT_SECRET</code> credentials. Grant that client <code>permission_read</code> with <code>realmScope: &quot;ownOrNull&quot;</code> for a single realm, or <code>&quot;any&quot;</code> when serving multiple realms. The default <code>&quot;own&quot;</code> grant cannot read global policy definitions. The built-in <code>system</code> client already has the required access.</p><p>Each authenticated HTTP request and socket connection fetches one catalog and combines it with the caller&#39;s introspected grants. All permission checks within that request/connection reuse the local evaluator. Catalog errors fail the request; there is no fallback to permission names. Catalogs are not cached across requests. Existing token-introspection caching and socket-session lifetimes still apply, so changes to grants are visible when those are refreshed.</p><p>Scope restricts what a caller can do, not who it is. Grants are only evaluated for tokens carrying the <code>global</code> scope; a token issued without it (e.g. a custom login client registered without <code>global</code>) still authenticates and keeps its identity, but is evaluated with <strong>no grants</strong>, so every permission check denies with 403. Policies that read the identity still see the real caller, and paths that authorize by identity rather than by permission — a node or analysis client reading its own credentials, the messenger mailbox, bucket ownership, realm socket rooms — still honour such a token. Authup applies the same rule since authup#3723. client-ui requests <code>global openid</code>, and Authup&#39;s client-credentials and password grants always issue <code>global</code>.</p><p>This preserves policy trees and grant realm scopes at the evaluator boundary. Existing endpoints that only perform a permission pre-check still perform a pre-check: this upgrade does not add row-policy checks or collection filtering to those endpoints. Restricting every entity read/write by resource attributes is a separate service-level change.</p><p>The frontend kit uses a single <code>POST /authorization/check</code> batch for UI gates and refreshes it at the policy expiry returned by Authup. Hub navigation follows those refreshes. Browser users do not need <code>permission_read</code> merely to render menus.</p><p>Authup&#39;s beta.67 upgrade also applies its folders/event-aggregate migration and pins database timestamps to UTC. Follow the <a href="https://github.com/authup/authup/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.67" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Authup upgrade notes</a> for existing databases that used local time. Beta.68 quotes newly serialized strings; Hub&#39;s text transformer reads both these and legacy unquoted messenger payloads, so no Hub data migration is needed.</p><h2 id="server-messenger-authenticates-against-authup" tabindex="-1">server-messenger authenticates against Authup <a class="header-anchor" href="#server-messenger-authenticates-against-authup" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;server-messenger authenticates against Authup&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking.</strong> server-messenger now verifies every REST request and socket connection against Authup, like the other services. Before, its REST surface never registered an Authup client and attached one fixed master-realm user to <strong>every</strong> request: no token was needed, and every caller read and acknowledged the same mailbox. Treat any message stored by an affected version as exposed.</p><p>What an operator must do:</p><ul><li>Grant the messenger&#39;s service client (<code>CLIENT_ID</code>, default <code>system</code>) <code>permission_read</code>, with the same <code>realmScope</code> guidance as in <a href="#authup-upgrade-to-beta-68">Authup upgrade to beta.68</a> — every authenticated request reads the authorization catalog. The built-in <code>system</code> client already has it.</li><li>Make sure <code>AUTHUP_URL</code>, <code>CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>CLIENT_SECRET</code> are set for the messenger. Every Hub service now fails closed without Authup: no identity is attached, so every authenticated route answers 401 and every socket connection is refused. The previous no-Authup fallback (a fixed master-realm user, plus a <code>dryRun</code> mode that granted every permission) is removed.</li><li>Callers (nodes) must send a bearer token on every request, including the <code>GET /messages/stream</code> SSE stream.</li></ul><h2 id="malformed-bearer-tokens-no-longer-authenticate" tabindex="-1">Malformed bearer tokens no longer authenticate <a class="header-anchor" href="#malformed-bearer-tokens-no-longer-authenticate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Malformed bearer tokens no longer authenticate&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Security fix, no operator action required in Hub.</strong> Authup up to <code>1.0.0-beta.69</code> falls back to the token in the introspection request&#39;s own <code>Authorization</code> header when the submitted <code>token</code> fails its validator (shorter than 16 or longer than 2048 characters). Hub&#39;s token verifier sends that header with the service&#39;s <strong>own</strong> credentials, so a malformed bearer — <code>Bearer x</code>, an oversized string, or a garbage <code>access_token</code> cookie — was introspected as the Hub service client (<code>active</code>, <code>global</code> scope, its grants) and every Hub service acted on it as that client.</p><p>Every Hub service now refuses a credential that is not structurally a compact JWS within those bounds <strong>before</strong> introspecting it, answering 401 (sockets: connection refused). The fallback itself is fixed upstream in authup#3724; upgrading Authup to the first release after <code>1.0.0-beta.69</code> closes the upstream half, until then the Hub-side guard is what stops it. Any token-verifier cache (Redis <code>token:*</code> keys) may hold entries for such strings — flush it once after upgrading.</p><h2 id="attaching-nodes-to-a-project-requires-project-update" tabindex="-1">Attaching nodes to a project requires <code>project_update</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#attaching-nodes-to-a-project-requires-project-update" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Attaching nodes to a project requires `project_update`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking for some roles.</strong> Adding a node to a project (<code>POST /project-nodes</code>) and removing one (<code>DELETE /project-nodes/:id</code>) both require <code>project_update</code>. Previously, adding required <code>project_create</code> <strong>and</strong> <code>project_update</code>. Holding only <code>project_create</code> now creates a project but cannot attach nodes to it; grant <code>project_update</code> to every role that creates projects with nodes.</p><h2 id="writing-analysis-buckets-and-files-requires-analysis-update" tabindex="-1">Writing analysis buckets and files requires <code>analysis_update</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#writing-analysis-buckets-and-files-requires-analysis-update" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Writing analysis buckets and files requires `analysis_update`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking for some roles.</strong> Analysis buckets and their files are authorized against the analysis&#39;s realm, not the caller&#39;s:</p><ul><li>Creating, updating or deleting an analysis bucket, and adding, updating or deleting a <strong>code</strong> file, require <code>analysis_update</code> reaching the analysis&#39;s realm (see <a href="#writes-take-their-realm-reach-from-realm-scope">Writes take their realm reach from <code>realm_scope</code></a>). Grant it to every role that uploads analysis code.</li><li>Code files are frozen once the analysis configuration is locked: a write answers 400, and so does deleting the code bucket.</li><li>An analysis has at most one bucket per type, and a storage bucket backs at most one analysis bucket (a second one answers 409).</li><li>Uploads made directly to server-storage are checked when server-core registers them: a code file must come from the analysis&#39;s realm while unlocked, a result or temp file from the realm of an approved analysis node or from the analysis&#39;s own client. A rejected upload is not registered. A rejected <strong>code</strong> upload is also deleted from storage (an unregistered code object would break every later build), so server-storage must consume the <code>storageBucketFileTasks</code> queue (it does from this release on); a rejected result or temp upload stays in storage, so a misjudged uploader never costs result data. This check is realm-level only, and uploads through storage by anyone else are rejected too; upload through a node or the owner realm.</li><li>Storage still lets an uploader delete its own object, even a code file of a locked analysis. server-core logs such a deletion as an error; it cannot prevent it.</li><li>A <strong>result</strong> or <strong>temp</strong> file may be added by a caller whose <code>analysis_update</code> reaches every realm (<code>realm_scope: any</code>, no policy — <code>admin</code>, <code>system</code>), by the realm of a node whose analysis participation is approved, or by the analysis&#39;s own client. Only a code file can be the root file.</li><li>Result files are updated and deleted by the analysis realm with <code>analysis_update</code>; temp files only by a caller whose <code>analysis_update</code> reaches every realm.</li></ul><h2 id="uploading-into-a-bucket-requires-ownership-or-bucket-update" tabindex="-1">Uploading into a bucket requires ownership or <code>bucket_update</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#uploading-into-a-bucket-requires-ownership-or-bucket-update" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Uploading into a bucket requires ownership or `bucket_update`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking for API clients.</strong> <code>POST /buckets/:id/upload</code> into a bucket created over HTTP (one without a <code>refId</code>) answers 403 unless the caller created the bucket or holds <code>bucket_update</code> with access to the bucket&#39;s realm. Previously any authenticated caller could upload into any bucket. Buckets created before the creator was recorded have no owner, so only <code>bucket_update</code> holders can upload into them. Analysis buckets (which carry a <code>refId</code>) are unchanged: their uploads are still checked by server-core when it registers them.</p><h2 id="reading-nodes-master-images-registries-registry-projects-and-events-requires-read" tabindex="-1">Reading nodes, master images, registries, registry projects and events requires <code>*_read</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-nodes-master-images-registries-registry-projects-and-events-requires-read" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reading nodes, master images, registries, registry projects and events requires `*_read`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking for every deployment.</strong> Listing or reading these entities now needs a read grant. Five permission names are new — <code>node_read</code>, <code>master_image_read</code>, <code>master_image_group_read</code>, <code>registry_read</code>, <code>registry_project_read</code> — and the existing <code>event_read</code> is now realm-scoped.</p><p><strong>Which permissions allow a read.</strong> Each read accepts its read name <strong>or</strong> any of the entity&#39;s write/manage names, since holding a write implies read:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Entity</th><th>Accepted permissions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>nodes</td><td><code>node_read</code>, <code>node_create</code>, <code>node_update</code>, <code>node_delete</code></td></tr><tr><td>master images</td><td><code>master_image_read</code>, <code>master_image_manage</code></td></tr><tr><td>master image groups</td><td><code>master_image_group_read</code>, <code>master_image_group_manage</code></td></tr><tr><td>registries</td><td><code>registry_read</code>, <code>registry_manage</code></td></tr><tr><td>registry projects</td><td><code>registry_project_read</code>, <code>registry_project_manage</code>, <code>registry_manage</code></td></tr><tr><td>events (server-telemetry)</td><td><code>event_read</code>, <code>event_delete</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>A caller holding none of them gets <strong>403</strong> — deliberately, with no fallback. The same table also gates:</p><ul><li><strong>Realtime</strong> — subscribing to node, master-image and master-image-group updates.</li><li><strong>Includes</strong> — a relation requested via <code>include</code> needs the related entity&#39;s permissions: <code>/nodes?include=registry</code> and <code>/registry-projects?include=registry</code> need a registry one, <code>/nodes?include=registryProject</code> a registry-project one.</li></ul><p><strong>Which rows a read returns.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Nodes, master images, master image groups and registries</strong> are global catalogs: the grant is a capability gate only, and every row stays readable across realms.</li><li><strong>Registry projects and events</strong> are narrowed by the grant&#39;s <code>realm_scope</code>: <code>own</code> returns the caller&#39;s realm, <code>ownOrNull</code> adds realm-less rows, <code>any</code> returns all. A grant carrying a policy Hub cannot turn into a query falls back to the caller&#39;s own realm.</li><li><strong>A node client</strong> reads its own registry project without any grant, on both <code>GET /registry-projects</code> (narrowed to that one project) and <code>GET /registry-projects/:id</code>. A read grant it is given applies as for any other caller.</li><li><strong>Record reads</strong> (<code>GET /&lt;entity&gt;/:id</code>) apply the same scope, so a row out of reach answers 404 exactly like a missing one. A malformed id (<code>GET /events/not-a-uuid</code>, <code>filter[id]=not-a-uuid</code>) answers 400.</li></ul><p>An audit event belongs to the realm of the row it records (a project event to the project&#39;s realm). Rows without a single <code>realmId</code> column — master images and groups, registries, and the project-node and analysis-node junctions (which carry one realm per side) — produce realm-less events, visible to <code>ownOrNull</code> and <code>any</code> grants only. <strong>Events stored before this release carry no realm</strong>, so an <code>own</code> grant does not see them; <code>ownOrNull</code> does (and with it every realm&#39;s old events).</p><p><strong>Before upgrading:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Add the new names to your Authup provisioning first.</strong> The built-in <code>admin</code> role (<code>*</code>) only picks up permissions that exist when provisioning runs.</li><li>Grant <code>node_read</code>, <code>master_image_read</code> and <code>master_image_group_read</code> to every role that creates or edits projects or views analyses — project creation lists nodes and master images.</li><li>Grant <code>registry_read</code> to roles that manage a node&#39;s registry connection (<code>node_update</code>) — the node&#39;s Registry tab lists <code>/registries</code> to pick one.</li><li>Make sure the worker&#39;s service client (<code>system</code>) has <code>node_read</code>, <code>registry_read</code> and <code>master_image_read</code>; <code>*</code> covers them once they are provisioned.</li></ol><p><strong>After upgrading:</strong> nothing to do for node clients. server-core re-grants each existing node&#39;s client its permissions (<code>analysis_read</code>, <code>node_read</code>, <code>analysis_node_log_create</code>, all at <code>realm_scope: none</code>) in the background — once at start, then hourly — so node-side services (pod orchestration, the storage service, the message broker) keep reading <code>/nodes</code>. Each pass removes any other permission a node client was granted by hand, as node saves always have. A node whose client cannot be updated is logged as an error and retried on the next pass. If <code>node_read</code> is not provisioned in Authup, every node is logged as failed until it is, and the next pass after provisioning grants it without a restart; creating a node fails meanwhile, instead of yielding a client that gets 403 later.</p><p><strong>Known gaps:</strong></p><ul><li>Registry projects joined through <code>include=registryProject</code> are not narrowed to the caller&#39;s realm reach.</li><li>Nested includes are not gated: <code>/project-nodes?include=node.registryProject</code> or <code>/analysis-nodes?include=node.registry</code> return the nested rows without the read permissions above, and <code>/nodes?include=registryProject.registry</code> checks only the first segment. Secret fields stay excluded.</li><li>Includes within the project/analysis family (<code>include=node</code>, <code>include=masterImage</code>) are not gated yet.</li><li>The registry-project realtime room still broadcasts every realm&#39;s projects to a subscriber holding <code>registry_manage</code> or <code>registry_project_manage</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="reading-analyses-requires-analysis-read-and-reach" tabindex="-1">Reading analyses requires <code>analysis_read</code> and reach <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-analyses-requires-analysis-read-and-reach" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reading analyses requires `analysis_read` and reach&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking for every deployment.</strong> <code>GET /analyses</code>, <code>GET /analysis-nodes</code>, <code>GET /analysis-node-events</code>, <code>GET /analysis-buckets</code> and <code>GET /analysis-bucket-files</code> (list and record) now need a grant and return only rows of the analyses the caller reaches.</p><p><strong>Which permissions allow a read.</strong> <code>analysis_read</code>, or any analysis write or lifecycle permission: <code>analysis_create</code>, <code>analysis_update</code>, <code>analysis_delete</code>, <code>analysis_approve</code>, <code>analysis_execution_start</code>, <code>analysis_execution_stop</code>. Holding none of them answers <strong>403</strong>. <code>analysis_result_read</code> alone no longer lists analyses; pair it with <code>analysis_read</code>.</p><p><strong>Which analyses a read returns.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Users</strong> see what their grant&#39;s <code>realm_scope</code> covers, <strong>plus</strong> every analysis in which a node of their realm takes part (pending or rejected approval included): <ul><li><code>any</code>: every analysis. The built-in <code>admin</code> role and the <code>system</code> client hold their grants at <code>any</code>.</li><li><code>own</code> / <code>ownOrNull</code>: analyses of their own realm. Analyses always carry a realm, so the two behave the same here.</li></ul></li><li><strong>The master realm is no longer special.</strong> A master-realm user whose grants are <code>own</code> sees only master&#39;s own analyses. Give cross-realm roles their analysis grants at <code>realm_scope: any</code>.</li><li><strong>A node&#39;s client</strong> sees exactly the analyses its node takes part in, and an <strong>analysis&#39; client</strong> only its own analysis. Neither gains anything from its realm or from its grants&#39; realm scope. New analysis clients are given <code>analysis_read</code>; clients of analyses created before the upgrade are not.</li><li><strong>A grant carrying a policy</strong> narrows every one of these reads by that policy, evaluated against the analysis the row belongs to.</li><li><code>GET /analysis-nodes</code> and <code>GET /analysis-node-events</code> return every row of a reachable analysis, so nodes see each other&#39;s approval and execution status.</li><li><strong>Buckets and their files depend on the bucket type:</strong><ul><li><code>CODE</code>: everyone who reaches the analysis, including a node realm whose approval is still pending (it needs the code to review it).</li><li><code>TEMP</code>: the reach of the grant, plus realms and node clients whose node is <strong>approved</strong>.</li><li><code>RESULT</code>: as <code>TEMP</code> for the bucket itself. Its <strong>files</strong> additionally need <code>analysis_result_read</code>, whose own <code>realm_scope</code> can only narrow that reach further, never widen it. Without it, result files are left out of listings (not refused) and a result file answers 404.</li></ul></li><li>A record out of reach answers <strong>404</strong>, like a missing one.</li><li><strong>Logs</strong> (<code>/analysis-logs</code>, <code>/analysis-node-logs</code>) used to need only a login, because server-core reads and deletes them with its own credentials: <ul><li><strong>Analysis logs</strong> are read with the read permissions above, by the reach of the grant plus realms and node clients whose node is <strong>approved</strong>.</li><li><strong>A node&#39;s logs</strong> are read with <code>analysis_update</code> or <code>analysis_approve</code>, by the reach of the grant (every node), or by that node itself (its realm&#39;s users or its client) once approved. A node does not see other nodes&#39; logs.</li><li><strong>Deleting</strong> either needs <code>analysis_update</code> through the reach of the grant; taking part as a node never allows it.</li><li>A malformed <code>analysisId</code>/<code>nodeId</code> answers <strong>400</strong>.</li><li><code>POST /analysis-node-logs</code> is unchanged.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Before upgrading:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Add <code>analysis_read</code> to your Authup provisioning.</strong> The built-in <code>admin</code> role (<code>*</code>) only picks up permissions that exist when provisioning runs. Until it exists, every node save fails (creating or editing a node, and assigning its registry), and every existing node client is logged as failed on each hourly re-sync.</li><li>Grant <code>analysis_read</code> to roles that view analyses without changing them, and <code>analysis_result_read</code> to roles that download results.</li><li>Grant the analysis permissions of every role that must see other realms&#39; analyses at <code>realm_scope: any</code>.</li><li>Make sure the worker&#39;s service client (<code>CLIENT_ID</code>, default <code>system</code>) holds <code>analysis_read</code> at <code>realm_scope: any</code>; otherwise builds and distributions of other realms&#39; analyses fail with 404. <code>system</code> is covered by <code>*</code> once the name is provisioned.</li></ol><p><strong>After upgrading:</strong> node clients need nothing. server-core grants each node&#39;s client <code>analysis_read</code> in the background, once at start and then hourly.</p><h2 id="writes-take-their-realm-reach-from-realm-scope" tabindex="-1">Writes take their realm reach from <code>realm_scope</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#writes-take-their-realm-reach-from-realm-scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Writes take their realm reach from `realm_scope`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking for every deployment.</strong> Hub no longer treats the master realm as special anywhere a record is written. Every create, update, delete and command — analyses, analysis nodes, buckets and files, nodes, projects, project nodes, registry projects, client and registry credentials, storage buckets and files, telemetry events — needs its permission through a grant whose <code>realm_scope</code> reaches the record&#39;s realm:</p><ul><li><code>any</code> reaches every realm. The built-in <code>admin</code> role and the <code>system</code> client hold their grants at <code>any</code>.</li><li><code>own</code> reaches the caller&#39;s own realm, <code>ownOrNull</code> also records without a realm (realm-less registry projects and telemetry events), <code>none</code> reaches nothing.</li><li>A master-realm user whose grants are <code>own</code> now writes only in the master realm. Grant cross-realm roles their permissions at <code>realm_scope: any</code>.</li><li>A record out of reach answers <strong>403</strong>. On an update, the grant&#39;s policies must allow both the stored and the updated record.</li></ul><p>Behaviour changes on top:</p><ul><li><strong>Analysis commands check a permission.</strong> Starting a build or a distribution needs <code>analysis_execution_start</code>; every other command (<code>buildCheck</code>, <code>distributionCheck</code>, <code>configurationLock</code>/<code>Unlock</code>, <code>storageCheck</code>) needs <code>analysis_update</code>. Before, only realm membership was checked.</li><li><strong>Node clients</strong> hold <code>analysis_read</code>, <code>node_read</code> and the new <code>analysis_node_log_create</code>, all at <code>realm_scope: none</code>, and no longer <code>analysis_update</code>. Their own node is authorized by binding: a node&#39;s client reports execution status on its own analysis-node rows, writes its run&#39;s logs and reads them, but cannot change other analyses of its realm. The background sync rewrites existing grants.</li><li><strong>Analysis clients&#39;</strong> permissions are created at <code>realm_scope: none</code>.</li><li><strong>A node&#39;s client is assigned by Hub only.</strong> <code>clientId</code> can no longer be set through <code>POST</code>/<code>PUT /nodes</code>, and a client belongs to at most one node. The upgrade migration keeps a client shared by several nodes on the oldest one; the others get a fresh client on their next save, so their node software needs the new credentials.</li><li><strong>Writing a node&#39;s logs</strong> (<code>POST /analysis-node-logs</code>) needs <code>analysis_node_log_create</code>: the node&#39;s own client, or a grant reaching the node&#39;s realm. Telemetry&#39;s <code>log_create</code> is not given to nodes.</li><li><strong>Registry credentials of a node</strong> need <code>node_update</code> or <code>registry_manage</code> reaching the node&#39;s realm.</li><li><strong>Deleting a realm-less telemetry event</strong> needs <code>event_delete</code> at <code>ownOrNull</code> or <code>any</code>.</li><li><strong>Creating an analysis</strong> needs <code>analysis_create</code> reaching the project&#39;s realm.</li></ul><p><strong>Before upgrading:</strong></p><ol><li>Add <code>analysis_node_log_create</code> to your Authup provisioning. Until it exists, every node save fails and every node client is logged as failed on each hourly re-sync.</li><li>Give every role that must write in other realms its permissions at <code>realm_scope: any</code>, and give roles that start builds or distributions <code>analysis_execution_start</code>.</li></ol><h2 id="realtime-and-rate-limiting-follow-realm-scope" tabindex="-1">Realtime and rate limiting follow <code>realm_scope</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#realtime-and-rate-limiting-follow-realm-scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Realtime and rate limiting follow `realm_scope`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Breaking for cross-realm users.</strong> The last master-realm-by-name checks are gone.</p><ul><li><strong>Realtime namespaces.</strong> A socket connects to <code>/resources/&lt;realmId&gt;</code> (that realm&#39;s events) or <code>/resources</code> (every realm&#39;s events). The own realm&#39;s namespace is open. Another realm&#39;s namespace and the global one need a hub grant at <code>realm_scope: any</code> with no policy.</li><li><strong>Realtime subscriptions</strong> check the room&#39;s permissions against the namespace: in a realm namespace a grant must reach that realm, in the global namespace it must reach every realm. An <code>any</code> grant for one entity no longer opens another entity&#39;s rooms.</li><li><strong>The UI</strong> connects to the namespace of the realm a list asks for, else of the current management realm (<em>Admin → Realms</em>, now offered on every realm). Master images, master-image groups, registries and registry projects are published to the global namespace only, so their lists update live only for callers with an unbounded grant — as before, when only the master realm got them. Before, every other user&#39;s connection was refused, so their lists updated only on reload.</li><li><strong>The rate limiter</strong> no longer throttles a client that holds <strong>every</strong> hub permission at <code>realm_scope: any</code> with no policy (the <code>system</code> client). Being in the master realm no longer exempts a client; users are always limited.</li></ul><h2 id="authup-upgrade-to-beta-69" tabindex="-1">Authup upgrade to beta.69 <a class="header-anchor" href="#authup-upgrade-to-beta-69" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Authup upgrade to beta.69&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Hub now requires Authup <code>1.0.0-beta.69</code>, a security release. Read the <a href="https://authup.org/guide/deployment/upgrading#security-hardening-that-changes-behaviour" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Authup upgrade guide</a> before upgrading. Hub needs no code or data changes; these points concern the Authup deployment Hub runs against:</p><ul><li><strong>Tokens are bound to their realm.</strong> On a Redis-backed Authup, drop the <code>oauth2_token_claims*</code> keys once after upgrading, or tokens verified before the upgrade keep bypassing the new check until they expire.</li><li><strong>Client reads are realm-gated.</strong> Hub&#39;s service client creates and reads the per-node and per-analysis Authup clients in the realm of that node or analysis. A Hub client whose <code>client_*</code> grants are <code>own</code> or <code>ownOrNull</code> no longer sees clients in other realms; grant <code>any</code> (the built-in <code>system</code> client already has it).</li><li><strong>HTTP Basic names resolve in the route realm or the master realm.</strong> Hub&#39;s Harbor webhook authenticates with the registry client&#39;s <strong>id</strong>, which resolves globally, so it is unaffected.</li><li><strong>Rate limiting is two-stage.</strong> Behind one NAT address (a clinic or office network) raise <code>rateLimitAnonymousMax</code>, and pin Authup&#39;s <code>trustProxy</code> to the hop count of your proxy.</li><li><strong>Some users sign in once more.</strong> Token cookies are now written <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, and <code>Secure</code> on https. Behind a TLS-terminating proxy, forward <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> (the <a href="./reverse-proxy.html">reverse proxy</a> example already does) so server-side cookie writes in client-ui get <code>Secure</code> too.</li></ul><h2 id="database-variables" tabindex="-1">Database Variables <a class="header-anchor" href="#database-variables" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Database Variables&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Used by <code>server-core</code>, <code>server-storage</code>, <code>server-telemetry</code>, and <code>server-messenger</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>DB_TYPE</code></td><td>Yes</td><td><code>mysql</code>, <code>postgres</code>, or <code>better-sqlite3</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>DB_HOST</code></td><td>Yes*</td><td>Database hostname</td></tr><tr><td><code>DB_PORT</code></td><td>Yes*</td><td>Database port</td></tr><tr><td><code>DB_USERNAME</code></td><td>Yes*</td><td>Database username</td></tr><tr><td><code>DB_PASSWORD</code></td><td>Yes*</td><td>Database password</td></tr><tr><td><code>DB_DATABASE</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Database name (or <code>:memory:</code> for SQLite)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>*Not required for SQLite.</p><h3 id="sqlite-has-no-upgrade-path" tabindex="-1">SQLite has no upgrade path <a class="header-anchor" href="#sqlite-has-no-upgrade-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;SQLite has no upgrade path&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Migrations only run for <code>mysql</code> and <code>postgres</code>. On SQLite the schema is built from the entity classes by <code>synchronize()</code>, and only when no schema exists yet — so an <strong>existing</strong> SQLite database is never migrated or altered on upgrade.</p><p>Schema changes therefore break a persistent SQLite deployment. The rename of the <code>analysis</code> table to <code>analyses</code> is one such change: a SQLite database created before it keeps the old table, and queries against the new name fail.</p><p>Use SQLite only for tests and throwaway/in-memory (<code>:memory:</code>) instances. For any database you intend to keep, use MySQL or PostgreSQL. To carry an existing SQLite database across a schema change, export its data and re-import it into a freshly created database.</p><h3 id="timestamps-are-stamped-and-read-in-utc" tabindex="-1">Timestamps are stamped and read in UTC <a class="header-anchor" href="#timestamps-are-stamped-and-read-in-utc" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Timestamps are stamped and read in UTC&quot;">​</a></h3><p>On MySQL and PostgreSQL every Hub service pins its database sessions to UTC (<code>time_zone</code> on MySQL, <code>TimeZone</code> on PostgreSQL), reads the zone-less <code>created_at</code> / <code>updated_at</code> columns back as UTC, and binds <code>Date</code> parameters in UTC — whatever timezone the database server or the host running Hub is set to. The same pin applies to the <code>migration</code> CLI. There is nothing to configure, and <code>DB_PIN_TIMEZONE</code> is not needed. A database and host already running in UTC (the container image sets no <code>TZ</code>) see no change.</p><p>Before, the database stamped these columns in its session timezone, the driver read them in the process timezone, and the messenger&#39;s expiry sweep compared its <code>expires_at</code> column against a <code>Date</code> bound in the process timezone, so the three only agreed when every clock ran in UTC.</p><p><strong>If the database itself ran in local time</strong>, rows it stamped before this upgrade hold local wall-clock values and now read as UTC, shifted by that offset. Rows written afterwards are correct. Check the database&#39;s zone before upgrading:</p><div class="language-sql vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sql</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">-- PostgreSQL</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">SHOW timezone;</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">-- MySQL</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SELECT</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> @@</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">global</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">time_zone</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, @@system_time_zone;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>If it is not UTC and the historical values matter, convert them <strong>while no Hub service is writing</strong>: stop every replica of the old version, run the conversion, then start the new version. Do not convert after the new version has started, or during a rolling upgrade where old and new replicas write side by side: the statements cannot tell a legacy local-time row from a correct UTC one, so they would shift the correct rows too. Convert each table and column once, with the zone the database used:</p><div class="language-sql vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sql</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">-- PostgreSQL</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">UPDATE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> events </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> created_at </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (created_at </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">AT</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> TIME</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> ZONE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;Europe/Berlin&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">AT</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> TIME</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> ZONE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;UTC&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">;</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">-- MySQL (needs the server&#39;s time zone tables loaded)</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">UPDATE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> events </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> created_at </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> CONVERT_TZ(created_at, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&#39;Europe/Berlin&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&#39;+00:00&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A database setting that contradicts the pin stops the boot with an error naming it: a MySQL <code>timezone</code> other than UTC, a MySQL <code>dateStrings</code> or <code>typeCast</code>, or a PostgreSQL <code>TimeZone</code> other than UTC in the driver&#39;s startup <code>options</code> (e.g. via <code>DB_DRIVER_EXTRA</code>). Remove it — half a pin would shift values instead of fixing them. A MySQL replication setup is refused the same way for now, since its per-connection session cannot be pinned yet.</p><h3 id="replicas-and-boot-time-migrations" tabindex="-1">Replicas and boot-time migrations <a class="header-anchor" href="#replicas-and-boot-time-migrations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Replicas and boot-time migrations&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each service applies pending migrations when it starts. On MySQL and PostgreSQL it now does so while holding a database lock named after the service (<code>server-core:migration</code>, <code>server-storage:migration</code>, …), so replicas that start at the same time apply the chain once: one runs it, the others wait and then find nothing pending. The <code>migration run</code> / <code>migration revert</code> CLI commands take the same lock, so a migration job and a booting replica cannot overlap either. server-core&#39;s Harbor registry setup (<code>HARBOR_URL</code>) is serialized the same way.</p><p>A replica that cannot get the lock within 60 seconds fails its boot instead of migrating unlocked; the orchestrator restarts it, and by then the chain has been applied. Each lock holds one pooled connection while the migration runs on another, so the connection pool needs at least two connections.</p><h2 id="storage-variables-server-storage" tabindex="-1">Storage Variables (server-storage) <a class="header-anchor" href="#storage-variables-server-storage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Storage Variables (server-storage)&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>MINIO_ENDPOINT</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>MinIO/S3 endpoint URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>MINIO_ACCESS_KEY</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>S3 access key</td></tr><tr><td><code>MINIO_SECRET_KEY</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>S3 secret key</td></tr><tr><td><code>MINIO_USE_SSL</code></td><td>No</td><td>Enable SSL for MinIO connection</td></tr><tr><td><code>MINIO_PORT</code></td><td>No</td><td>MinIO port</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="telemetry-variables-server-telemetry" tabindex="-1">Telemetry Variables (server-telemetry) <a class="header-anchor" href="#telemetry-variables-server-telemetry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Telemetry Variables (server-telemetry)&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>VICTORIA_LOGS_URL</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>VictoriaLogs endpoint URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td>No</td><td>Days an audit/event row is kept before the daily sweep removes it (default <code>7</code>; <code>0</code> = keep forever)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="frontend-variables-client-ui" tabindex="-1">Frontend Variables (client-ui) <a class="header-anchor" href="#frontend-variables-client-ui" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Frontend Variables (client-ui)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The full list lives in the <a href="./../../reference/frontend/#environment-variables">frontend reference</a>. Two of them are deployment decisions rather than service addresses, and go together:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NUXT_PUBLIC_COOKIE_DOMAIN</code></td><td>No</td><td><code>Domain</code> attribute for the UI&#39;s session cookies. <strong>Leave empty.</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>NUXT_PUBLIC_AUTHUP_COOKIE_PREFIX</code></td><td>No</td><td>Namespace prefixed onto every session cookie name. Set this <strong>whenever <code>NUXT_PUBLIC_COOKIE_DOMAIN</code> is widened</strong>, so Authup&#39;s own hosted pages can&#39;t collide with the UI&#39;s cookies on that domain.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Setting a prefix is only safe once the backend agrees on it: <code>server-core</code>, <code>server-storage</code>, <code>server-telemetry</code> and <code>server-messenger</code> each fall back to reading the <code>access_token</code> cookie (for requests that can&#39;t carry an <code>Authorization</code> header — a stream download is a top-level navigation) when the identity middleware finds nothing else. That fallback needs the <strong>same</strong> prefix, via each service&#39;s own <code>COOKIE_PREFIX</code> env var (see <a href="#common-variables">Common Variables</a>) — set it to the exact same value as <code>NUXT_PUBLIC_AUTHUP_COOKIE_PREFIX</code> / <code>COOKIE_PREFIX</code>, verbatim (no separator is inserted automatically on either side).</p><h3 id="where-authup-is-served-matters" tabindex="-1">Where Authup is served matters <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-authup-is-served-matters" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where Authup is served matters&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The UI and Authup&#39;s own hosted pages persist their sessions under the <strong>same cookie names</strong>. If both can see each other&#39;s cookies, they hydrate, rotate and revoke each other&#39;s tokens, and the user is logged out on the next page reload.</p><p>Empty (host-only) cookies are correct in every layout. Setting a <code>Domain</code> delivers the cookies to every subdomain of that value — including Authup&#39;s host, if it sits below it — in which case also set <code>NUXT_PUBLIC_AUTHUP_COOKIE_PREFIX</code> to keep the two cookie sets apart. Serving Authup on a path of the UI&#39;s own origin no longer needs either variable as of Authup <code>1.0.0-beta.64</code> (<a href="https://github.com/authup/authup/issues/3495" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">authup#3495</a>): the consoles scope their own cookies to that sub-path automatically.</p><p>The layout matrix and the upgrade caveat are documented under <a href="./../../reference/frontend/#session-cookies">Session cookies</a>.</p>',95)])])}const p=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
